[init_ensemble_plan_agent]: Use Pattern A as a very light “multi-leg + blend” layer, but since there is only one provided script, create diversity by keeping the exact same skrub DataOps training chain and instantiating a small set of parallel prediction legs that differ only in training snapshot, then merge only at the prediction level.

The simplest effective version is a 3-leg self-ensemble:
- leg 1 = the current LightGBM solution exactly as-is
- leg 2 = same code path, but trained on a bootstrap sample of `train_part` (sample rows with replacement)
- leg 3 = same code path, but trained on a complementary/subsampled view of `train_part` (for example 85–90% without replacement, stratified if easy)

This keeps the original `predictor_lgbm(data_train, target_col)` structure intact for every leg:
- each leg still does `skrub.var("data", ...)`
- each leg still uses `TableVectorizer()`
- each leg still uses the same fixed `LGBMClassifier(...)`
- no rewrite of preprocessing/modeling flow
- only a merge layer is added after `make_learner(fitted=True)`

For merging, prefer probability blending over hard-vote blending:
- call `predict_proba({"data": valid_part})`
- align class columns using `learner.classes_`
- average class probabilities across legs
- final prediction is `argmax` of the averaged probability matrix

If `predict_proba` becomes awkward in the current learner object, fallback to majority vote on `predict`, but soft averaging is better and still easy.

Recommended weighting:
- start with equal weights `[1/3, 1/3, 1/3]`
- if you want one tiny extra improvement without complexity, give the full-data leg slightly higher weight, e.g. `[0.5, 0.25, 0.25]`
This is robust because the original leg is the strongest/most stable, while the resampled legs contribute variance reduction.

Implementation sketch:
- keep the existing holdout split unchanged
- define a tiny helper that builds one learner from any training dataframe by reusing `predictor_lgbm`
- prepare:
  - `train_part_full = train_part`
  - `train_part_boot = bootstrap(train_part)`
  - `train_part_sub = subsample(train_part, frac=0.9)`
- fit one learner per training slice
- get validation probabilities from each learner on the same `valid_part`
- weighted-average probabilities
- map back to class labels
- compute the same `accuracy_score`

Why this is the best merge plan here:
- there is only one submitted solution, so classic cross-solution ensembling is unavailable
- bootstrap/subsample bagging is the cleanest way to create diverse legs without touching the internal skrub pipeline
- it is lightweight: only 2 extra fits
- it respects the “do not modify original solutions too much” rule
- it avoids hyperparameter retuning, alternative model integration, or complex stacking machinery

A good optional extension, still simple:
- add test-time augmentation style averaging by predicting each leg both on the raw `valid_part` and on a row-shuffled copy only if needed for stability checks, but this is optional and usually unnecessary
- better optional extension instead: use a 2-leg ensemble if runtime is tight:
  - leg 1 full data
  - leg 2 bootstrap data
  - blend 0.7 / 0.3

Avoid stacking with a meta-model here:
- with only one base architecture family and one holdout split, stacking is more fragile than useful
- it adds leakage risk and code complexity
- bagged probability averaging should give most of the attainable ensemble gain for almost no engineering cost

So the final recommendation is:
- keep the existing LightGBM + TableVectorizer skrub pipeline unchanged
- clone it into 2–3 independent learners trained on lightly different row samples
- merge with weighted average of class probabilities
- use the full-data leg as the anchor with the highest weight
- report the holdout score from the blended predictions only
